arm64: dts: h713: use the stock property names on the motor node

motor_control_fdt_parse (stock vmlinux 0xc05d62c8) looks up its properties
by name, and two of them are spelled with underscores: "active_level"
(string at 0xc11618ec, read at 0xc05d6314) and "motor_type" (0xc1161a03,
read at 0xc05d6434).  Everything else is hyphenated: motor-phase-num,
motor-step-num, motor-step-type, motor-cycle, motor-phase-udelay,
motor-step-mdelay, motor-gpio-phase%d, limiter-up-gpio, limiter-dn-gpio,
motor-cw-table, motor-ccw-table.  The stock DTB spells them the same way
(hy310-board.dts:2635).

This port invented "active-level" and "motor-type" and taught only its own
driver to read them, so the node did not satisfy the "motor-control"
binding it claims in its second compatible.  Use the names the binding
actually defines.  The driver accepts both spellings, stock name first, so
this patch and the driver patch are independent of each other.

The comment above the node is corrected at the same time.  The stock DTB
has exactly one motor node and the stock kernel exactly one motor driver
(kallsyms c05d5c1c..c05d7d14, globals motor_ctl / pMotordev), and the stock
GPIO dump lists no motor lines besides PH4..PH7 and PH14 -- so this is the
projector's focus drive, not a keystone motor sitting next to one.  It also
records what PH14 actually is: a range gate that reads active across the
allowed travel, with a pull-down on stock, not an end-stop contact.

No values change; the built DTB differs only in those two property names.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1915,10 +1915,27 @@
 	 */
 
 	/*
-	 * Keystone motor driver (hy310-keystone-motor).
-	 * Stepper motor for projector keystone correction.
-	 * 4-phase half-step sequence, 1 limit switch (PH14, active HIGH).
-	 * Values extracted from stock hy310-board.dtb via raw FDT parsing.
+	 * The projector's single stepper motor: the focus drive.
+	 *
+	 * Every value below is the stock DTB's, node "motor_ctr" in
+	 * re/vendor/HY310/extracted/dtb_extracted/hy310-board.dts:2635.
+	 * That DTB has exactly one motor node and the stock kernel has
+	 * exactly one motor driver, so this is not a keystone motor
+	 * beside a focus motor -- it is the focus motor.  The old name
+	 * came from the HY300 tree, not from this firmware.
+	 *
+	 * The property names are the ones motor_control_fdt_parse
+	 * (stock vmlinux 0xc05d62c8) actually looks up, including the
+	 * two spelled with underscores.
+	 *
+	 * PH14 is a range gate, not an end stop: it reads active over
+	 * the whole allowed travel, and the driver reverses when it
+	 * goes inactive (motor_run_up_control 0xc05d6ea0).  On stock it
+	 * is a GPIO input with a pull-down, which is the idle level for
+	 * active_level = 1 (re/captures/HY310-DEV/stock_pinconf_pio.txt,
+	 * "pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down").  The driver sets that
+	 * bias itself, so no pinctrl group is needed here.
+	 *
 	 * GPIO format: <&pio bank pin flags>  bank 7 = PH
 	 */
 	motor_ctr: motor-ctr {
@@ -1932,9 +1949,9 @@
 		motor-gpio-phase3 = <&pio 7 7 0>;	/* PH7 */
 
 		limiter-up-gpio = <&pio 7 14 0>;	/* PH14 */
-		active-level = <1>;			/* active HIGH */
+		active_level = <1>;			/* limiter active HIGH */
 
-		motor-type = <0>;			/* 0 = stepper */
+		motor_type = <0>;			/* 0 = stepper */
 		motor-step-num = <8>;			/* 8 half-steps per sequence */
 		motor-cycle = <2>;			/* 2 cycles per mstep */
 		motor-step-type = <8>;
@@ -1943,8 +1960,8 @@
 		motor-cw-table = [01 09 08 0A 02 06 04 05];
 		motor-ccw-table = [05 04 06 02 0A 08 09 01];
 
-		motor-phase-udelay = <1>;		/* 1ms between phases */
-		motor-step-mdelay = <1>;		/* 1ms between steps */
+		motor-phase-udelay = <1>;		/* ms between phases */
+		motor-step-mdelay = <1>;		/* ms between steps */
 	};
 
 	/*
